{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggXzszvSAXqju4DDLBsa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Conclusion: these three bands can't be fitted reliably with the octahedral d⁷ ligand-field model, so I'm not giving values for Dq, B or C.**\n\nFor high-spin d⁷, the three spin-allowed bands are ν₁ (⁴T₁g→⁴T₂g), ν₂ (→⁴A₂g) and ν₃ (→⁴T₁g(P)). I used the Tanabe–Sugano matrix for the two ⁴T₁g states, with ⁴T₁g(F) at –6Dq, ⁴T₁g(P) at 15B + 6Dq and an off-diagonal term of 6B. This gives:\n\n- ν₂ – ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₃ = 2d, where d = √[(7.5B + 6Dq)² + 36B²]\n- ν₁ = 2Dq – 7.5B + d\n\nI tried the two reasonable assignments.\n\n1. **ν₁ = 9 460 and ν₂ = 20 160.** This gives 10Dq = 10 700 cm⁻¹ (Dq = 1 070) and B ≈ 800 cm⁻¹. It then predicts ν₃ ≈ 26 700 cm⁻¹, far from the observed 21 160 cm⁻¹, so it fails.\n2. **ν₁ = 9 460 and ν₃ = 20 160 or 21 160, with ν₂ unobserved.** This gives Dq ≈ 1 150 cm⁻¹ but B ≈ 390–450 cm⁻¹. That is only about 40–45% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (about 970 cm⁻¹), which is physically unreasonable.\n\nThe 20 160 and 21 160 cm⁻¹ bands are only 1 000 cm⁻¹ apart. That spacing looks like a spin–orbit or spin-forbidden (doublet) component, or a split or distorted band, rather than separate ν₂ and ν₃ bands. In a regular octahedron, ν₃ should lie well above ν₂. The assignment is therefore ambiguous, and the model can't fix it.\n\nI also can't determine C. It would need spin-forbidden transitions, and none are assigned here.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"The three bands fit no consistent octahedral d7 assignment: ν2 and ν3 are only 1000 cm-1 apart, and every fit gives either a ν3 far from observed or an unphysically low B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1659,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1659,"ephem